#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921" :question/problem "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787a7445-1cb7-5ac5-950f-51a380eb447f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317913} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c42555-612f-5d59-97c5-5290598a877c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,-4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317914} {:answer/id #uuid "44ddfb1d-f321-51e2-bdef-7bb63f8adccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317915} {:answer/id #uuid "233189a9-7cc7-5e9b-803f-c63e072b07b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317916} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e54162-e89b-5dbb-ae32-c766417ba3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,4,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ddfb1d-f321-51e2-bdef-7bb63f8adccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317915} :answer-field/id #uuid "69826181-e839-534d-8f04-811418b3d660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0aedac8-4550-5f55-b4ac-09ba9ac8ed13" :question/math-academy-id "q-129169" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3z-11=0 \\\\ 2x-2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6faec47-b0e1-53cc-a122-ba924137a87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,8,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309472} {:answer/id #uuid "239ff84d-7ca6-561a-8043-85e62e7c8a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309473} {:answer/id #uuid "79bb4a2d-dafd-5be4-af5a-4aa992b2f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309474} {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} {:answer/id #uuid "f344b47d-af36-5ae8-8c3d-e30f4f54e1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0daf3085-8d89-5e14-92e1-e117adc2c4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f626f64-b605-58e9-8e55-f56146c3feb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129170" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b41fa7c-383b-551d-8e7d-195af093e233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309486} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef7e40-b70b-5a85-8a24-375e22f4f302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309487} {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} {:answer/id #uuid "2f47da4a-b316-542d-9add-3c553c97e434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309489} {:answer/id #uuid "eca8e996-c8fa-5d8f-b034-7e46ddd5ba4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8149ef53-3f86-5b48-a7e1-8fa0afbc8615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "923148c0-0ecb-5f91-a025-b18771e50157" :question/math-academy-id "q-129224" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "792797aa-dac9-5b13-8d48-232f5a08494b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=4,\\,b=⟨-1,-7,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317920} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc03c30-6c94-5d79-9ea3-0d3bab7e87e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-8,\\,b=⟨-7,5,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317921} {:answer/id #uuid "aad9b1ce-b023-529c-9ca6-2465308721f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8,\\,b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317922} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb79c25-6570-5656-8f4b-0aa89eb79556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-5,-1,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317923} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d9707f-bbe3-5514-b6c7-4435b3b448b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=7,\\,b=⟨-8,-5,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aad9b1ce-b023-529c-9ca6-2465308721f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8,\\,b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317922} :answer-field/id #uuid "891db865-96ab-540e-b237-c18f5e352742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d143e7a-cebc-5440-b9bb-3b483458d2cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129229" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+3y-2z-8=0$ and $2x+y-3z-16=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\nx+0-2z-8=0\\,⟹\\,x=2z+8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=2z+8$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2z+8)+0-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ 4z+16-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2(0)+8=8$ and $a=⟨8,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+3y-2z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,3,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y-3z-16=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,3,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-7i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨8,0,0⟩+t⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8+0=8,$ and $b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2255437b-2a7c-5d4b-ad0d-c5342e87f421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317927} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ec6e5-63a1-5ba1-aca6-2d683cb7adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928} {:answer/id #uuid "b2667c7c-92a3-53f3-b37b-df98b05ec599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317929} {:answer/id #uuid "df20b430-25c3-5035-a27e-d4983f0fe92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317930} {:answer/id #uuid "1997b8a8-bf75-5ad1-9f36-d3435660bf7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2255437b-2a7c-5d4b-ad0d-c5342e87f421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317927} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d2c490-607d-5fd2-bf7b-1eb2ec0dceb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6407551-67a9-560e-b723-72e719f9cb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bc9956-3f2a-5751-9955-380cc068e4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb4e7da-d423-537d-b6d1-eca94ff0ffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309501} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd6c990-9990-5e92-9979-5092fd03374b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309502} {:answer/id #uuid "6a28c4a1-3a4b-5b47-8e2a-096224035b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309503} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6f1dbf-64e4-5fbc-ab2f-a0d761ede179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0531b19e-756e-5c97-abd0-1f5e648bd0cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129259" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac37965-8c39-5fe8-8477-dca2f06947a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317934} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baef66-cb25-55f8-9dcb-524cc89acc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317935} {:answer/id #uuid "4967c162-3d17-5ddc-89c4-29461e0fb076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317936} {:answer/id #uuid "434a6c0a-9995-57aa-ae40-2f5688e38d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317937} {:answer/id #uuid "d0910555-34fd-5736-bb97-1bfe04b7cec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0910555-34fd-5736-bb97-1bfe04b7cec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ddbfadd-a8b1-5f1f-ad22-809f6ba2beaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22e39499-5747-5698-af95-b4fc0c4d8191" :question/math-academy-id "q-129261" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} {:answer/id #uuid "b091c44e-c006-5b37-85b6-190cdc188a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146877} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fe2a3a-8c62-5389-a161-a25f6e8a33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146878} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2bcc97-cf0e-5657-8010-ae978b880459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146879} {:answer/id #uuid "68a96f68-b656-59fb-9345-0525fd9ebd33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} :answer-field/id #uuid "57427c13-e607-5012-b842-cb76e784fdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddee53f3-5b0d-5dc0-9b9e-825db292f3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71110" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e0282b-1ec5-59c0-991e-d4722c73d1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146882} {:answer/id #uuid "9435a61d-73df-56b5-9486-ff3d4b07c3fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146883} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b5f61d-4e6a-52c9-a280-c4704b0d3ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146884} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cb7009-f93e-5f14-b754-9576e41c0fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146885} {:answer/id #uuid "0516b784-54ac-59d2-b858-747472940f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9435a61d-73df-56b5-9486-ff3d4b07c3fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146883} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1ad179-1e0f-5c64-ac47-b005f4a39e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8314953-42cd-5ac5-902d-92a3c570bac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71112" :question/problem "Given the two planes $-x-2y+z=0$ and $2x+4y-2z+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y-2z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z+1=0 \\\\ x+2y-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ -x-2y+z-\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98dad773-e343-5720-b6d0-06acff1fcc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146894} {:answer/id #uuid "2df7b237-0cf7-5db4-a613-d9911a551947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146895} {:answer/id #uuid "37ff0265-8d39-5cfc-b9a1-4b42b3ef6549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-5, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146896} {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} {:answer/id #uuid "669966ee-f3cb-5ad2-81fa-197cc11629e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} :answer-field/id #uuid "50693ecf-036a-5c40-a007-20ea1b1a1626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c01a5d8f-f2ce-5a5d-bfd0-e648dd7fcc1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71187" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0ce8b6-6dbe-5dea-9b88-5729aa59ac33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-5,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309521} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d6712-803b-5fda-86ca-914e95f9f438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20,25,20⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309522} {:answer/id #uuid "3b15c3cb-3433-5846-8d12-1c834085ece6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309523} {:answer/id #uuid "147e4708-d07d-5614-ae6d-4ca7d55b1f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309524} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ec77c1-ae66-5a52-b343-ea088b631874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,-4,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d6712-803b-5fda-86ca-914e95f9f438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20,25,20⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309522} :answer-field/id #uuid "887dd48c-ca9b-539c-a987-adbe261b4e19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13385c8f-4361-5ebf-99b7-4f21bd7e90b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71266" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d3777c-931e-54be-bc36-4a01094158c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528} {:answer/id #uuid "2976cdff-3ec8-5aeb-9e6f-7febfe55f031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309529} {:answer/id #uuid "823c751c-6086-558b-a81f-7377b09e6e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} {:answer/id #uuid "570af63d-4fcd-572a-8beb-b6e23ef63681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309531} {:answer/id #uuid "3d216364-6877-5cf5-b473-f039b81e2723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "823c751c-6086-558b-a81f-7377b09e6e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e29a774-9bd2-54dd-8c69-555bcacd95c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce5171f6-22a8-5be0-9547-0d96404368c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71268" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $z=1$ and $x=y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $z=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=y$ (or $x-y=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f532964-059a-56c1-8751-a27bf1f4829e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317941} {:answer/id #uuid "87d707d9-d5f1-53ed-a5e1-0f9e11a83245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317942} {:answer/id #uuid "34a73aaa-8712-5c38-ba6a-d74ec3011a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317943} {:answer/id #uuid "eb89f0ce-7a3f-5319-a7ee-579a4fbb60eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317944} {:answer/id #uuid "708b3e8a-7f69-5439-a602-c231abac9e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a73aaa-8712-5c38-ba6a-d74ec3011a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317943} :answer-field/id #uuid "b744509a-aaf3-5773-a166-8fa33cb468e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd7b78c1-6586-5772-840d-06dfdf6ee176" :question/math-academy-id "q-71269" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $x=1$ and $y=-z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $x=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y=-z$ (or $y+z=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} {:answer/id #uuid "ada6b8f6-5526-5e24-ad84-0b05b1181052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317949} {:answer/id #uuid "bce644e9-3d29-5fa1-af4d-33771a072e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950} {:answer/id #uuid "e881c0c4-9a27-5d88-9335-7ba7bebb29d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317951} {:answer/id #uuid "2e11eee1-0155-5d84-96b6-03d1ea939615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddf3ab9-17c6-51d5-a9ad-06d3404c0068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61cf4d39-9683-533e-9394-f4988b6cbe81" :question/math-academy-id "q-71446" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308325ff-1cef-5051-a5e8-80a4f61c1912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317955} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a1fc4-4222-51fe-b3da-0ee4c2222007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317956} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3bf136-3ab6-5529-91cb-fe72aa416075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317957} {:answer/id #uuid "05509d3f-017b-515f-81bf-22b6415a40b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} {:answer/id #uuid "5422e736-9203-5e36-af7d-46408e9f0d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05509d3f-017b-515f-81bf-22b6415a40b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} :answer-field/id #uuid "db363893-de21-563c-b1a8-bb84a9a85da9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0212fc2-f78c-514d-ac95-d9fd26dc0ceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}]}
